WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.168
-Our first guest tonight
is a best-selling author

00:00:03.168 --> 00:00:05.105
and Emmy-winning host
of "All In with Chris Hayes,"

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:07.006
which airs weeknights at 8:00
on MSNBC.

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:09.942
He also hosts the podcast
"Why Is This Happening?"

00:00:09.942 --> 00:00:11.110
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.880
our good friend Chris Hayes,
everybody!

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.615
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:21.888 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:28.127 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:30.029
Hi! I'm so happy
to have you here.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:31.497
-It's great to be here.

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:34.000
-It's also --
This used to happen more

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.069
with the previous guy in office,
but news breaks during the day.

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:39.738
And I'm very lucky to have
a newsman here with us,

00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:41.908
because news just broke
the Trump Organization

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:45.945
was found guilty
on all 17 counts of tax...

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.279
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:49.315
Hey! Hey! Hey!!

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.651
Do you know how hard it is
to do your taxes?!

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:55.388
To get it right on every form?!

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:56.823
-They just like justice.

00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:59.258
[ Laughter ]
It's good. Yes, all 17 counts.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:01.894
I mean, I think it was
a pretty open-and-shut case.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:03.763
-It did seem
pretty open-and-shut.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.564
-They had all the documents.
It clearly --

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:07.167
I mean, basically
what it amounted to

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.134
was they engineered
a bunch of ways

00:01:09.134 --> 00:01:12.638
to pay benefits and perks
to the CFO, Allen Weisselberg,

00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:14.607
that they were not reporting
as taxes.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:16.976
And, again, this is one of
those, like, you know,

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:19.479
"cutting corners" kind of things
that's just clearly illegal.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:21.214
-Yep.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:23.116
-Anyone that is, like,
working with a lawyer,

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:25.017
an accountant, or has,
like, a sense of integrity

00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:26.919
is like,
"No, you don't do that."

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:29.088
You report your taxes.
-Yeah.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:32.925
-But that does not apply
to the Trump Org, obviously.

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:35.194
As now has been proven --

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:36.829
you know, found by
a jury of his peers.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:38.264
-We should stress,
this is not --

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.266
Donald Trump has not been
found guilty of anything.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.101
This is the Trump Organization.
-No, that's right.

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:43.970
I was gonna make a joke
that the jury of the peers

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.137
was, like, I guess

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:46.839
other real-estate companies
in New York.

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:48.608
They were, like, sitting
in the jury box.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:50.810
Yes, this was the very rare

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.446
and rarely seen criminal trial
of a corporation,

00:01:54.446 --> 00:01:56.616
which is the Trump Org.
-Which I would imagine that,

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.684
based on the news of the day,
Trump either says

00:01:58.684 --> 00:02:00.753
he has everything to do with
or nothing to do with it.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.788
-Yeah, that's exactly right.
-Today might be a day

00:02:02.788 --> 00:02:04.924
where he's like,
"I barely have heard of it."

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.659
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:07.793
-It's another Trump.

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:09.262
-"It could be
a different Trump."

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.430
-Might be Don Jr.'s company.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.733
-So that is interesting.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.268
Obviously, there will be
a lot to unpack about that.

00:02:16.268 --> 00:02:18.271
Today, of course, the reason
we're happy to have you

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:20.072
is it's the end of the midterms.

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:22.275
Tonight is the Georgia run-off.
-That's right.

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.010
-This is a fascinating race.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.579
What is your --
Where do you see it?

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.314
Based on the outcome, what
are you going to glean from it?

00:02:29.314 --> 00:02:31.217
-I mean, I think
there's two big --

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:34.286
I mean, one is,
will Georgians have, like,

00:02:34.286 --> 00:02:37.757
an extremely competent
and impressive U.S. senator?

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:38.857
-Yeah.

00:02:38.857 --> 00:02:40.259
-Or will they have
Herschel Walker?

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:42.328
[ Laughter ]
Like -- Like, you know --

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:46.933
And I don't say that as any
kind of ideological judgment.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.868
It's just that Herschel Walker

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:51.637
is the nominee
to be the senator of Georgia

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:53.539
because he was
on "Celebrity Apprentice."

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.041
-Yes.
-And because at a certain

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:57.743
point, people were talking
about the Georgia election,

00:02:57.743 --> 00:02:59.913
and Donald Trump was like,
"I know someone from Georgia.

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:01.381
-Yeah.

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:02.715
-Herschel Walker
is from Georgia."

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:04.650
And literally the Republican
establishment in Georgia

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.753
was like,
"No, he lives in Dallas.

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:09.522
He's lived in Dallas
for 20 years."

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:11.757
And Trump was like,
"No, that's Herschel Walker."

00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:13.659
-Right, because Donald Trump
I think --

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:15.294
It' very easy
to connect the dots

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:16.962
that he remembered
Herschel Walker

00:03:16.962 --> 00:03:19.832
as a Georgia football player,
excellent one,

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:21.167
and then knew him as a USFL guy.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.068
-And then he was on
"Celebrity Apprentice."

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:24.637
-He was on
"Celebrity Apprentice."

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:25.971
So there -- those are the --

00:03:25.971 --> 00:03:27.372
And really not
a whole lot of dots.

00:03:27.372 --> 00:03:28.941
-Those are
the three qualifications

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:30.910
for him to be the senator.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.912
And so there's
a real question of --

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:34.981
It is one of -- Of all
the races I've ever covered,

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.917
one of the starkest
juxtapositions.

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:39.285
-Of just candidate quality.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.720
-Candidate quality.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.688
-And then the second thing
I think really is,

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:44.490
after a brutal midterms

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.225
for Donald Trump's
political brand,

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:50.162
for candidates associated with
him and handpicked by him,

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:53.899
I mean, he will have managed,
if Warnock pulls this off,

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:56.569
to basically have cost Georgia

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.771
three Senate seats in two years.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.540
-Yeah.
-Which I think is not

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.708
gonna be helpful for him
in the case

00:04:02.708 --> 00:04:04.710
that he's trying to build
to run for president again.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.047
-And it should be noted
that the governor of Georgia,

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:10.483
the Republican governor of
Georgia, won his race easily.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:11.784
-Yes.
-This is not a state

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:15.254
that is swinging wildly
to become a blue state.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:17.523
-No. In fact, if you look
at some of the other states,

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:20.759
like, for instance,
Pennsylvania and Arizona

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:23.195
and Michigan particularly,
like, they seemed to move more

00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:26.065
in the Democrats' direction
down the ticket line.

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.500
Georgia kind of swung back.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.935
The big outlier is

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:31.003
Raphael Warnock
against Herschel Walker.

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:33.005
And the reason is that
Herschel Walker

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:34.573
has no business
being a U.S. senator.

00:04:34.573 --> 00:04:37.877
-Yeah.
-And enough marginal voters

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:40.012
in Georgia were aware of that

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:41.981
to give Warnock the advantage
the first time around.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:43.582
We'll see if that holds tonight.

00:04:43.582 --> 00:04:46.052
-It will be an interesting
night as we see those come in.

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:49.355
You talk about, is the impact

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:51.524
of Donald Trump
in the Republican Party waning?

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:53.893
It definitely seems like
things are not going as well

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:56.695
as they have in the past,
obviously.

00:04:56.695 --> 00:04:58.231
But I still don't see

00:04:58.231 --> 00:05:01.667
how he doesn't have
a massive amount of control

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:03.569
over the Republican Party
because of his base.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.638
-Oh, he definitely has
a massive amount of control.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:07.206
I mean, I think
two things are true.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:10.209
One is that he is still the
de facto leader of the party.

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:12.778
Two, that he's as
politically weak as he's been

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:15.948
basically since he came down
the escalator in 2015.

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:19.218
And, you know, I think
if the last five years --

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:20.819
-It is funny
that that's a true thing

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:21.954
but also really funny.

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:23.389
-[ Laughs ] Yeah. Yes.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.723
Everybody was like,
"Wait. What?"

00:05:24.723 --> 00:05:26.392
And they were like,
"Oh, yeah. That's what he did."

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:28.194
-That was actually --
-That's what he did.

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:32.030
It's so funny, too, instead
of, like, elevating to meet us,

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.233
he was like,
"I shall come down to you."

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:35.701
-Yes.

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:37.336
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:39.638
In, like, the lobby.
That was the other part of it.

00:05:39.638 --> 00:05:41.507
-And we were like,
"Ha-ha-ha! You goon!"

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.509
And then he was president.
-Yes, exactly.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:45.310
[ Laughter ]
That's exactly what happened.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.313
-Which -- I shortened it up.
I sort of condensed it.

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:49.582
-But those are the main parts
of the story.

00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:51.117
-He came down, we all laughed,

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:52.885
and then he was president
for four years.

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:55.087
[ Laughter ]
He did the full four.

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:57.189
-That's exactly what happened.

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:58.691
And because of that,
I think, like,

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.059
it's always so risky to draw
some straight line, you know,

00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:03.796
some trend that's happening
and just draw it
out into the future.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.464
And I think, again, the last
five years has taught us,

00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:07.400
like, between Donald Trump
and, like, COVID,

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.101
you never know
what's going to happen.

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:10.969
I have no idea
what the future will bring.

00:06:10.969 --> 00:06:14.173
He might be very strong.
Right now he is very weak.

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:16.876
And the reason he's weak
isn't because people

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:18.945
are morally appalled by the fact

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:21.080
that he's, like,
dining with white nationalists

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.348
and anti-Semites.

00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:24.050
It's because he's a loser
and he keeps losing

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:26.685
and he keeps screwing
things up for them.

00:06:26.685 --> 00:06:28.220
-Yes.
-And they --

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.122
[ Cheers and applause ]
They don't like that,

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:31.823
and they want to be rid of it,

00:06:31.823 --> 00:06:34.060
but they're not quite sure
how to be rid of it.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.961
-Well -- One thing I want
to say is, you know

00:06:35.961 --> 00:06:39.065
I think a lot of candidates try
to follow the Trump playbook.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:42.601
And this will seem insincere
as I say it, but I do mean it.

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:45.604
I do think it's harder to be
Donald Trump than it looks.

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:47.039
-Absolutely.
-I think that he has

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:50.976
a unique combination of skills
that if other people try it,

00:06:50.976 --> 00:06:54.146
it just seems
weirdly inauthentic.

00:06:54.146 --> 00:06:56.482
And with him, you're like,
"That's exactly who he is."

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:59.552
-I absolutely --
I 100% agree with that.

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:03.455
I think his -- The schtick
he has is his schtick.

00:07:03.455 --> 00:07:05.724
And no one else
can really do that schtick.

00:07:05.724 --> 00:07:07.926
Even when they try to do it,
it doesn't work, "A."

00:07:07.926 --> 00:07:10.163
"B," it's hard to be --
[ Laughs ]

00:07:10.163 --> 00:07:12.497
I don't want to
remote diagnose the man.

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:17.270
But, like, if you have,
like, a fake sociopath

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.473
and a real sociopath
going against each other,

00:07:20.473 --> 00:07:23.009
like, the real sociopath
has a real advantage.

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:24.710
[ Laughter ]

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.345
And I do think that, like,

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.915
it's genuinely difficult
for most people to be

00:07:29.915 --> 00:07:32.618
as, essentially,
sociopathic as he is.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:34.287
So I think that schtick
doesn't work.

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:36.221
And then, I mean --
you see him with these --

00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:38.390
Everyone else just comes off
as creeps and weirdos.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:39.992
Blake Masters.

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:42.261
-Yes. This is in Arizona.
-In Arizona.

00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:44.797
You know, he had this two-minute
video of him shooting --

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:45.965
Did you see this?
-Yes.

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:47.533
-Shooting the gun
with a silencer.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:51.069
Just out there being like,
"Silencers get a bad rap, man."

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:53.739
It's like, "Whoa. Buddy."
Like...

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:55.908
I don't know what you're doing

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:58.310
or why you're running for office
on this platform.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.346
But all of the sort of reactions
people had to Trump,

00:08:01.346 --> 00:08:03.983
the fundamental thing
I think to remember is,

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.318
in almost, like,
the original ancient Greek

00:08:06.318 --> 00:08:08.721
meaning of the word,
he has charisma.

00:08:08.721 --> 00:08:10.423
-He really does.
-He does have charisma.

00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:12.391
And charisma
is a hard thing to fake.

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:13.625
And not everyone has it.

00:08:13.625 --> 00:08:15.728
And I actually think
even if you go back

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:18.530
and look at tape of him
in 2015, 2016,

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:20.298
that version of him
had a lot more charisma

00:08:20.298 --> 00:08:21.834
than the version now.

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:24.036
-Yeah.
-Like, significantly more.

00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:26.071
And that's a sui generis thing.

00:08:26.071 --> 00:08:28.040
It's hard to bottle.
It's hard to replicate.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:31.644
-Well, it'll be interesting
to watch him try to get it back.

00:08:31.644 --> 00:08:33.445
I have a million more questions
for you. Stick around.

00:08:33.445 --> 00:08:35.715
We'll be back with more
from Chris Hayes.

